From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anas <assosbs9@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 12:43:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Mac front end: DM_SCREEN=main opens the game on the screen
 with the menu bar

NX Library starts the game with no DM_SCREEN, so its window opened on the screen of the last focused window: Tears of the Kingdom on the MacBook's own screen while the player plays on the external one set as main display (MetalFX then scaled its 1080p picture to 2940x1654). main picks the first of NSScreen.screens, the one with the menu bar.

Upstream-Status: fork-only
Reason: games opened on the other screen than the one the player set as main
Issue: LNPANDY/NX2Apple#214

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 dm/host/front_mac.m   | 14 ++++++++++++++
 docs/env_switches.tsv |  1 +
 2 files changed, 15 insertions(+)

diff --git a/dm/host/front_mac.m b/dm/host/front_mac.m
index c95aeed..5db275e 100644
--- a/dm/host/front_mac.m
+++ b/dm/host/front_mac.m
@@ -1547,6 +1547,20 @@ void front_run(void (*guest)(void)) {
         if (g_hud_shown) LOG("front end: Metal performance HUD on (DM_METAL_HUD)");
         v.layer = g_layer; [(DmGameView *)v fitDrawable];
         [win center];
+        // DM_SCREEN=<NSScreenNumber>: the launcher's display. Moved before the window is shown and before any fullscreen request, so DM_FULLSCREEN enters fullscreen there
+        const char *want_scr = getenv("DM_SCREEN");
+        if (want_scr && *want_scr) {
+            NSScreen *on = nil; char *end = NULL; unsigned long id = strtoul(want_scr, &end, 10);
+            if (end != want_scr && !*end) for (NSScreen *sc in NSScreen.screens) if ([sc.deviceDescription[@"NSScreenNumber"] unsignedLongValue] == id) on = sc;
+            // NX2Apple: DM_SCREEN=main, the screen with the menu bar (System Settings → Displays → the main display), whichever screen had focus at launch: a launcher with no
+            // DM_SCREEN of its own (NX Library) left the game on the screen of the last focused window, often not the one the player plays on
+            if (!strcmp(want_scr, "main") && NSScreen.screens.count) { on = NSScreen.screens.firstObject; id = [on.deviceDescription[@"NSScreenNumber"] unsignedLongValue]; }
+            if (on) {
+                NSRect vf = on.visibleFrame, wf = win.frame;
+                [win setFrameOrigin:NSMakePoint(NSMidX(vf) - wf.size.width / 2, NSMidY(vf) - wf.size.height / 2)];
+                LOG("front end: window on screen %lu '%s' (DM_SCREEN)", id, on.localizedName.UTF8String);
+            } else LOG("front end: DM_SCREEN=%s ignored, no connected screen has that number", want_scr);
+        }
         [win makeKeyAndOrderFront:nil];
         screen_refresh(win.screen ?: NSScreen.mainScreen);
         g_win = win;
diff --git a/docs/env_switches.tsv b/docs/env_switches.tsv
index 153ce64..1aa3959 100644
--- a/docs/env_switches.tsv
+++ b/docs/env_switches.tsv
@@ -325,6 +325,7 @@ DM_SAVED_CHOICES	switch	`0` ignores the player's saved choices (launcher.<VAR> i
 DM_SAVE_SYNC	player	Cloud saves: the folder whose `<title ID>/save.dmsync` the save is synced through, `icloud` for iCloud Drive/nxnative Saves (the Mac); `0` or unset, off. The Mac launcher sets the folder chosen in its settings window's Cloud saves, the iPhone app the one picked in its launcher (dm/host/save_sync.h)
 DM_SAVE_SYNC_CHOICE	switch	Cloud saves: set by a front end that asks the player when the cloud's save and this device's both changed (any value: a save that changed on both sides is replaced on neither without a choice); `ask`: no choice yet, `cloud:<hash>` brings in the cloud's save with that hash, `local:<hash>` sends this device's over it (`local:none`: the cloud had none), `offline`: the cloud could not be read before the start, so the host does not wait for it; unset, the newer save wins (dm/host/save_sync.c, docs/frontends.md)
 DM_SCHEME_NAME	switch	Set by `build_ipa.sh` itself for xcodegen's `project.yml`: the Xcode scheme name, `DmIOS`, or `<display name> (<title ID>)` for a project prepared with `--prepare-xcode`
+DM_SCREEN	player	`=<number>` opens the Mac window on the connected screen with that `NSScreenNumber` (`CGDirectDisplayID`), centred, before any `DM_FULLSCREEN` request; an unknown number is logged and ignored. The NX2Apple launcher sets it to its own window's screen; `main` is the screen with the menu bar (the main display in System Settings)
 DM_SCREEN_ASPECT	player	`=<width/height>` (1.3 to 1.77, e.g. `1.43` for the iPhone Duo) renders a game whose profile has `screen_aspect on` in that shape: output 1920 wide, 1280-wide layout space, the HUD at the taller edges and 16:9's sides in view; `auto` (iOS) the app window's own shape when it is taller than 16:9, else 16:9; `0` or unset is 16:9; launcher setting
 DM_SCREEN_ASPECT_SITES	diagnostic	`=<mask>` with DM_SCREEN_ASPECT: which of the profile's layout-size conversion stubs follow the taller layout, one bit each (default all)
 DM_SCREEN_FOV	diagnostic	`off` with DM_SCREEN_ASPECT: the scene keeps the game's vertical angle (narrower sides) instead of 16:9's sides with more above and below
